991:Stop pretending. It was your idea to have today's contest, right?
992:...Eh? How did you know?
993:Is it supposed to be hard to guess? You're the only one who would come up with a strange idea like that.
994:Haha, okay. You got me.
995:Riu Kujou has guessed right. When I met Mr. Makiki outside the shrine several hours ago, I already asked him why he had come and gave him some advice.
996:...So did Chiori tell you that Riu is here?
997:Several hours ago
998:Yes. I had some contact with the lady Kujou served during the three years that she was in high school. I know that they grew up together and that Kujou doesn't want to leave her, but Chiori Mikami hopes that Kujou can go to college and give herself more options in the future.
999:...But given Riu's personality, she wouldn't agree to leave Chiori even if Chiori encouraged her to do so herself.
1000:That's right, but because I was her homeroom teacher, I want to try one more time.
1001:Thank you for caring about her.
1002:Do you think you can help me persuade her?
1003:Mr. Makiki, I know that you have good intentions, but neither you, me, nor Chiori can make decisions for Riu in regard to what she wants to do or what kind of person she wants to be in the future.
1004:So that's what you think... Sigh, am I the one who is wrong?
1005:How about I set up a channel for you two to communicate and find a method that you can both accept to help you understand each other's thoughts better? Maybe you'll both learn something that you didn't know about each other. What do you think?
1006:A different way of communicating... What do you have in mind?
1007:That's what happened. See? I didn't actually do anything. The main reason is that you and Mr. Makiki had a misunderstanding. He wanted you to be the best version of yourself, and that's also what you want from yourself. You just have a different idea of how you'll achieve that.
1008:Going to college isn't just about learning certain professional skills. It's also an environment and platform for young people who have just become adults to train themselves, broaden their horizons, and meet many good friends...
1009:Okay, that's enough. Are you possessed by Mr. Makiki or something...? I know all that.
1010:I don't actually hate college, but Miss Chiori is still in middle school, and this is a time when she really needs someone to be with her. Mr. and Mrs. Mikami are both busy with work and can't keep her company. If I'm also gone, how lonely would Miss Chiori be...?
1011:When she says this, Riu Kujou looks a little downcast, which is uncharacteristic of her. Suddenly, I realize that this conundrum can only be resolved by the person at the center of it.
1012:Riu, have you considered that Chiori will need to go to college one day?
1013:...
1014:...?
1015:...!!! {var:Shake}
1016:Riu Kujou comes to a sudden realization.
1017:You're right! As a professional maid, I will have failed my duties if I don't have a full understanding of that in advance!!!
1018:So will you consider finding out what it's like for yourself first? Some of my good friends are at the National University of Fuuchi. Why don't we play mahjong with them one day so you can take the opportunity to ask them what it's like on the campus...? Hey, why are you looking at me like that?
1019:{var:player_nickname}, I'm starting to understand why Miss Chiori treats you differently.
1020:But what will happen to Miss Chiori? If I really go to college...
1021:I'm still here, aren't I? I can play with her... Argh!
